# The expression knot: precedence, atoms, and the two bracketed literals
# (design.md §4.14 operators, §4.11 UFCS, §4.6 `?`, §4.3 literals, §4.9 calls
# and containers, §4.5 leading-dot variants, §4.16 holes).
#
# ## The map this file owes its reader (CLAUDE.md §11, 2026-08-16)
#
# **This is one module because the language leaves no choice.** Heroes refuses
# module cycles — `module_cycle` fires on the `use` edge, whatever it carries —
# and these functions call each other in a ring. In the Rust bootstrap the same
# code is five files (`expr.rs`, `primary.rs`, `control.rs`, `stmt.rs`,
# `name_stmt.rs`, 1025 lines) held together by `super::`; there is no such
# spelling here, so the knot is one file. §11's threshold yields for exactly
# this reason (author instruction *"go past the rule for heroes"*), and what it
# asks in exchange is this map.
#
# **The ring**, and it is why nothing here can be split: `parse_expr` → `binary`
# → `unary` → `postfix` → `primary` → (`group`, `array_literal`, `map_literal`,
# `case_expr`, `if_expr`, `match_expr`) → `block` → `statement` → back to
# `parse_expr`. `postfix` reaches `call_args`, which reaches `parse_expr`;
# `match_expr` reaches `arm`, which reaches both `statement` and `block`;
# `annotated` reaches `parse_type` in another module, which reaches nothing here
# — the one edge that leaves the knot and the reason `parse_type` stays its own
# file. `arm` reads its patterns with `parse/arm_pattern`, which since defect
# 124 reaches nothing here either, and refuses a declaration as its body with
# `parse/arm_body`, which reaches nothing here; nor do `parse/annotation`,
# `parse/margin_arms` and `parse/broken_arm`, which `annotated`, `match_expr`
# and `arm` ask what a failed type, a misplaced arm or a broken one owes, nor
# `parse/colon_habit`, `loop_habit`, `braced_arms` and `unclosed`: the `:`
# habit, `for`'s and `do`'s, arms in braces, a bracket the lexer found open.
#
# **The entry points**, and they are the whole public surface:
#
# | function | what it reads |
# |---|---|
# | `parse_expr` | one expression, weakest binding first |
# | `call_args` | `(x, name: y, @l)`, shared by calls, records and cases |
# | `statement` | one statement, its line end consumed |
# | `block` | an indented body — a declaration's, a loop's, an arm's |
# | `eat_python_colon` | the `:` habit, eaten with its fix, wherever a block opens |
#
# **The three line shapes that begin with a name**, told apart by one token of
# lookahead exactly as §4.4 designed it, and the whole difficulty of the
# statement half:
#
#     x = 5            name  =        immutable binding
#     v: i64 @ 0       name  :        declaration (the type is mandatory)
#     v @ v + 1        place @        mutation
#
# That third line is why the type is mandatory on a mutable declaration: a typo
# (`totl @ total + x`) must not silently declare a new variable, and the shapes
# have to be decidable without scanning the block — §1.3's locality test.
#
# ## One rule that shows up three times
#
# **Inside brackets, a newline is a separator, not a statement end.** The lexer
# keeps planting terminators at line ends even inside `(` `[` `{` (panel 007),
# and §4.9 makes newline the separator of a multi-line literal — so every list
# here skips terminators, and the array and map literals accept a newline AS the
# comma. A line end may stand before every closer and every `,` (panel 180 R1);
# where one stands before what would have gone on with the line above,
# `parse/line_end` says so (R3), and `parse/list_line` does for a literal (R2),
# as `parse/arm_line` does for the `-` that opens an arm (defect 123).

use token
use ast
use cursor
use lex_interp
use parse/type
use parse/line_end
use parse/list_line
use parse/arm_line
use parse/arm_pattern
use parse/arm_body
use parse/opening
use parse/swap_reading
use parse/annotation
use parse/margin_arms
use parse/broken_arm
use parse/operator_arm
use parse/headless
use parse/brace_habit
use parse/unclosed
use parse/swallowed
use parse/loop_habit
use parse/colon_habit
use parse/braced_arms
use parse/braced_lines
use parse/statement_end
use parse/refused_literal
use parse/heads
use parse/orphans
use parse/argument_marker
use parse/at_prefix
use operators

## The precedence table — §4.14, read from the bottom up

# What a token is as a binary operator: which one, and how tightly it binds.
# A miss means the token is not one, and the miss is **loud** — the caller asks
# `.is_err()` and stops climbing.
record Operator
    op: ast.BinaryOp
    power: i64

# **One function, and the reason is a defect this port created and panel 067
# caught.** Rust's `expr.rs` has a single `binary_op(kind) -> Option<(BinaryOp,
# u8)>`; the first version of this port split it in two — a power table and an
# operator table — and the split, not §4.7's ban, is what produced the twenty
# lines of padding that started that sitting. Worse, the second table had to
# invent an operator for ~50 tokens that are not operators, and it invented
# `.add`. The compiler-engineer proved what that costs: changing one line from
# `.percent => .rem` to `.percent => .add` — the copy-paste ten `.add` lines in
# a row invite — left `heroes check` at exit 0 and every test passing, with `%`
# parsing as `+`. Nothing in the language or the tests could see it, because
# both tables were internally consistent and only their *agreement* was the
# invariant, and nothing held it.
#
# Joined, that invariant is unrepresentable: there is one arm per operator
# carrying both halves, and a token with no answer says so.
#
# Two properties of the table are choices rather than accidents. Comparisons sit
# ABOVE `&&`, so `a < b && c < d` needs no parentheses. And the bitwise three sit
# between the comparisons and the shifts, each on its own level — C's order,
# which Go, Rust, Java and JavaScript all kept. **C's own mistake is the other
# one**: `&` binding looser than `==`, so `x & 1 == 0` means `x & (1 == 0)`.
# Heroes does not inherit it.
function binary_op(kind: token.TokenKind) -> Operator?
    return match kind
        .or_or   => ok(Operator(op: .or, power: 1))
        .and_and => ok(Operator(op: .and, power: 2))
        .eq_eq   => ok(Operator(op: .eq, power: 3))
        .bang_eq => ok(Operator(op: .ne, power: 3))
        .lt      => ok(Operator(op: .lt, power: 3))
        .le      => ok(Operator(op: .le, power: 3))
        .gt      => ok(Operator(op: .gt, power: 3))
        .ge      => ok(Operator(op: .ge, power: 3))
        .pipe    => ok(Operator(op: .bit_or, power: 4))
        .caret   => ok(Operator(op: .bit_xor, power: 5))
        .amp     => ok(Operator(op: .bit_and, power: 6))
        .shl     => ok(Operator(op: .shl, power: 7))
        .shr     => ok(Operator(op: .shr, power: 7))
        .plus    => ok(Operator(op: .add, power: 8))
        .minus   => ok(Operator(op: .sub, power: 8))
        .star    => ok(Operator(op: .mul, power: 9))
        .slash   => ok(Operator(op: .div, power: 9))
        .percent => ok(Operator(op: .rem, power: 9))
        # Everything else is not a binary operator, and says so rather than
        # answering. These are the arms §4.7's ban costs — one list now instead
        # of two, and each one a refusal rather than an invented `.add`.

        .ident | .int_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .str_piece | .char_lit => fail(
            code: "not_an_operator",
            msg: "a value is not an operator"
        )
        .kw_constant | .kw_function | .kw_record | .kw_variant | .kw_match => fail(
            code: "not_an_operator",
            msg: "a declaration keyword is not an operator"
        )
        .kw_if | .kw_else | .kw_for | .kw_while | .kw_in => fail(
            code: "not_an_operator",
            msg: "a control keyword is not an operator"
        )
        .kw_break | .kw_continue | .kw_return | .kw_test | .kw_assert => fail(
            code: "not_an_operator",
            msg: "a statement keyword is not an operator"
        )
        .kw_extern | .kw_nullptr | .kw_use | .kw_true | .kw_false | .kw_fail => fail(
            code: "not_an_operator",
            msg: "a keyword is not an operator"
        )
        .eq | .at | .colon | .colon_colon | .comma | .dot | .question | .hole => fail(
            code: "not_an_operator",
            msg: "punctuation is not an operator"
        )
        .arrow | .fat_arrow | .lparen | .rparen => fail(code: "not_an_operator", msg: "punctuation is not an operator")
        .lbracket | .rbracket | .lbrace | .rbrace => fail(code: "not_an_operator", msg: "a bracket is not an operator")
        .bang | .tilde => fail(code: "not_an_operator", msg: "a prefix operator is not a binary one")
        .comment | .terminator | .indent | .dedent | .eof | .error => fail(
            code: "not_an_operator",
            msg: "layout is not an operator"
        )

## Entry point 1 — one expression

function parse_expr(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    return binary(@c, @a, text, 0)

# Does this operand carry a block, so that the expression ends with it, or
# could it not be read, so that `while >` is not refused twice (defect 131)?
#
# ENUMERATED, never a catch-all: the day a new expression kind carries a
# block, this must answer for it, and a `_ => false` would answer "no"
# silently — which is the shape panel 060 paid for in the formatter.
function ends_the_expression(a: ast.Ast, id: i64) -> bool
    return match a.exprs[id].kind
        .if_expr | .match_expr | .error                                                => true
        .int_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .char_lit | .bool_lit | .null_ptr => false
        .name | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field | .field_name | .index               => false
        .call | .method | .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr                    => false

# Everything at `min_power` or tighter. All of Heroes' binary operators are
# left-associative, so the recursive call asks for `power + 1`.
function binary(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, min_power: i64) -> i64
    left: i64 @ unary(@c, @a, text)

    # A CONTROL FORM ENDS THE EXPRESSION, and this is panel 007's rule
    # enforced instead of remembered: *"at bracket depth zero every line's
    # indentation is structural — a long expression is broken inside
    # parentheses or not at all"* (Amendment B, and `design.md:1794` in
    # the same words).
    #
    # An `if` or a `match` in expression position carries a BLOCK, and
    # parsing that block consumes the dedent that closes it — so the next
    # token looks adjacent to the operand when it is on a different line,
    # at a shallower indent, and belongs to the next statement. Climbing
    # from there builds a continuation the language does not have.
    #
    # Measured before the guard (defect 005, `docs/work/DONE.md`): `return match c` with
    # arms, then `+ "x"` at the function's own indent, parsed as
    # `return (match … + "x")` and the program PRINTED `bx` at exit 0.
    # `heroes fmt` refused that file for a week and was right to — there
    # is no canonical spelling of a form that should not exist, which is
    # how the defect was found.
    #
    # `out + match c` is untouched: there `left` is `out` and the control
    # form is the RIGHT operand, which is the only side its block can be
    # on (`fmt.hero`'s `binary_control_tail` says the same thing
    # from the printer's side).
    if ends_the_expression(a, left)
        return left
    climbing: bool @ true

    while climbing
        found = binary_op(cursor.current_kind(c))

        if found.is_err()
            climbing @ false
        else
            operator = found.must()

            if operator.power < min_power
                climbing @ false
            else
                _ = cursor.bump(@c)
                right = binary(@c, @a, text, operator.power + 1)
                span = token.Span(start: a.exprs[left].span.start, end: a.exprs[right].span.end)
                left @ ast.push_expr(
                    @a.exprs,
                    ast.Expr(kind: .binary(op: operator.op, left: left, right: right), span: span)
                )
                climbing @ a.exprs[right].kind != .error

    return left

## `-x`, `!x`, `~x`

# Each takes exactly one operand and none is overloadable; `!` accepts only
# `bool`, which is a type judgment (§4.14: there is no truthiness).
function unary(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    k = cursor.current_kind(c)

    if k != .minus && k != .bang && k != .tilde
        return postfix(@c, @a, text)
    op: ast.UnaryOp @ .neg

    if k == .bang
        op @ .not

    if k == .tilde
        op @ .bit_not
    start = cursor.bump(@c).span
    operand = unary(@c, @a, text)
    span = token.Span(start: start.start, end: a.exprs[operand].span.end)
    return ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .unary(op: op, operand: operand), span: span))

## The tightest level — the suffixes

# A field, a call, an index, an error propagation — chained in whatever order
# they were written, which is what makes `l.text.slice(from: a, to: b)?[0]` read
# left to right.
function postfix(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    base: i64 @ primary(@c, @a, text)

    # **A suffix never reaches across a block that just closed.** `.ok v =>
    # match v` with the inner arms indented under it: the inner `match` consumes
    # its own arms and its dedent, and then this loop saw the `.` of the NEXT
    # OUTER ARM and read it as a field. The error landed on the following arm, a
    # line the author did nothing wrong on (panel 035).
    #
    # **Every suffix, not only the `.`** (defect 119, panel 181). The dedent is
    # a line end outside brackets, which ends the statement, so a `(`, a `[`, a
    # `?` or a `::` there heads a line of its own: `f = if c` … `else` …
    # `triple` over `(5)` ran as a call of the `if`'s value and printed 10,
    # where the reader sees a block and then a line. `(` and `[` begin a
    # statement, which is what the parser now reads; `?` and `::` begin none:
    # the lexer refuses them where the block's last line ends with a value,
    # handing this parser the joined line (`open_line`), and after a `return`,
    # `break` or `continue` the parser refuses them as a line no statement
    # begins with.
    if cursor.previous_kind(c) == .dedent
        return base
    chaining: bool @ true

    while chaining
        k = cursor.current_kind(c)

        if k == .dot
            base @ after_dot(@c, @a, text, base)

            if cursor.current_kind(c) == .dot && base < 0
                chaining @ false
        else if k == .colon_colon
            base @ after_colon_colon(@c, @a, text, base)
        else if k == .lparen
            start = a.exprs[base].span
            arguments = call_args(@c, @a, text)
            span = token.Span(start: start.start, end: cursor.previous_significant_span(c).end)
            base @ ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .call(callee: base, args: arguments), span: span))
        else if k == .lbracket
            bracket = c.pos
            _ = cursor.bump(@c)
            index = indexed(@c, @a, text, open: bracket)
            close = unclosed.end_index(@c, text: text, open: bracket)
            span = token.Span(start: a.exprs[base].span.start, end: close.end)
            base @ ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .index(base: base, index: index), span: span))
        else if k == .question
            question = cursor.bump(@c).span
            span = token.Span(start: a.exprs[base].span.start, end: question.end)
            base @ ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .try_expr(value: base), span: span))
        else
            chaining @ false

    return base

# `x.f(y)` is UFCS, `x.f` is a field read: the `(` decides (§4.11).
function after_dot(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, base: i64) -> i64
    _ = cursor.bump(@c)

    if !cursor.at(c, .ident)
        if !cursor.at_reported_error(c)
            cursor.error(
                @c,
                code: "expected_field_name",
                message: "expected a field or function name after `.`, found " + cursor.found(c, text),
                span: cursor.current_span(c)
            )
        return base
    name = cursor.bump(@c).span
    start = a.exprs[base].span

    if cursor.at(c, .lparen)
        arguments = call_args(@c, @a, text)
        span = token.Span(start: start.start, end: cursor.previous_significant_span(c).end)
        return ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .method(receiver: base, name: name, args: arguments), span: span))
    return ast.push_expr(
        @a.exprs,
        ast.Expr(kind: .field(base: base, name: name), span: token.Span(start: start.start, end: name.end))
    )

# `Point::x` — a record's own name on the left, a field's name on the right,
# and the whole expression is that field's name as a `str` (panel 132). It is
# NOT `after_dot`'s business: `.` reads a field FROM A VALUE and qualifies a
# module, and giving it a third meaning would make the line legible only to a
# reader who knows whether the base names a type or a value.
#
# No call may follow, because there is nothing to call: `Point::x(…)` is
# caught by the postfix loop as a call on a `str`, and says so.
function after_colon_colon(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, base: i64) -> i64
    _ = cursor.bump(@c)

    if !cursor.at(c, .ident)
        if !cursor.at_reported_error(c)
            cursor.error(
                @c,
                code: "expected_field_name_after_colons",
                message: "expected a field name after `::`, found " + cursor.found(c, text) + " — `Point::x` is the name of `Point`'s field `x`",
                span: cursor.current_span(c)
            )
        return base
    name = cursor.bump(@c).span
    start = a.exprs[base].span
    return ast.push_expr(
        @a.exprs,
        ast.Expr(kind: .field_name(base: base, name: name), span: token.Span(start: start.start, end: name.end))
    )

## The atoms

function primary(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    s = cursor.current_span(c)
    k = cursor.current_kind(c)

    # `@v` in a value is the sigil habit, read as the name (`parse/at_prefix`).
    if k == .at && at_prefix.in_value(@c, text)
        return primary(@c, @a, text)

    if k == .lparen
        return group(@c, @a, text)

    if k == .lbracket
        return array_literal(@c, @a, text)

    if k == .lbrace
        return map_literal(@c, @a, text)

    if k == .dot
        return case_expr(@c, @a, text)

    if k == .kw_if
        return if_expr(@c, @a, text)

    # `switch`, whose certain swap is `match`, is read as the swap writes it
    # (defect 133's class): its arms are arms, and their mistakes are told.
    if k == .kw_match || k == .error && swap_reading.reads_as(c, text) == .kw_match
        return match_expr(@c, @a, text)

    if k == .str_piece
        return interpolation(@c, @a, text)

    # A literal the lexer refused is read as the literal it stands for, so
    # the expression goes on past it: `f(0X10 + )` is refused for its `)`.
    simple = atom_kind(refused_literal.read_as(c, text))

    if simple.is_err()
        if !cursor.at_reported_error(c) && !line_end.said_here(c)
            cursor.error(
                @c,
                code: "expected_expression",
                message: "expected an expression, found " + cursor.found(c, text) + " — a value, a name, a call, `[`, `{`, `.case`, `if`, `match`, or `???`",
                span: s
            )

        # Not consumed: the statement parser decides where the line ends.
        return ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .error, span: s))
    _ = cursor.bump(@c)
    return ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: simple.must(), span: s))

# `f"a {n} b"`: the pieces are the tokens and the holes are parsed between them
# (panel 121 R1, R4). One node carries the whole literal's span with the holes
# as its children, so every walk sees them. IN THE KNOT AND NOT IN A MODULE OF
# ITS OWN, because it calls `parse_expr` (R5): a helper that calls back into
# this file closes a `use` cycle, and the knot clause of CLAUDE.md §11 is what
# lets the decided ceiling move by these lines with the reason written.
function interpolation(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    first = cursor.bump(@c)
    pieces: [token.Span] @ [first.span]
    holes: [i64] @ []
    last: token.Span @ first.span

    while !lex_interp.ends_closed(cursor.slice_of(text, last))
        holes @ holes.push(parse_expr(@c, @a, text))

        if !cursor.expect(@c, text: text, kind: .str_piece, code: "expected_hole_close", what: "`}` to close the hole")
            break
        last @ cursor.previous_significant_span(c)
        pieces @ pieces.push(last)

    return ast.push_expr(
        @a.exprs,
        ast.Expr(kind: .interp(pieces: pieces, holes: holes), span: token.Span(start: first.span.start, end: last.end))
    )

# The one-token atoms. `fail` is a name like any other here: `fail(code, msg)`
# is a call, and the compiler knows the callee (§4.6). Keeping it out of the
# grammar keeps the grammar smaller.
function atom_kind(k: token.TokenKind) -> ast.ExprKind?
    if k == .int_lit
        return ok(.int_lit)

    if k == .float_lit
        return ok(.float_lit)

    if k == .str_lit
        return ok(.str_lit)

    if k == .char_lit
        return ok(.char_lit)

    if k == .kw_true || k == .kw_false
        return ok(.bool_lit)

    if k == .kw_nullptr
        return ok(.null_ptr)

    if k == .hole
        return ok(.hole)

    if k == .ident || k == .kw_fail
        return ok(.name)
    return fail(code: "not_an_atom", msg: "no one-token expression starts here")

# The index after the `[` at `open`; where the lexer named the `[` never
# closed at the line end it stands on, or before the cursor, none is read and
# none is told missing, the lexer's report the mistake's one message.
function indexed(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, open: i64) -> i64
    if unclosed.never_closed(c, text, open: open) && (unclosed.ends_at_the_line(c) || !unclosed.in_reach(c, open: open))
        return ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .error, span: c.tokens[open].span))
    return parse_expr(@c, @a, text)

# `(e)` — grouping only. There is no tuple type, so a parenthesised expression
# is exactly its contents; `()` as a VALUE does not exist either (§4.3: `()` is
# the type of no value).
function group(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    at = c.pos
    _ = cursor.bump(@c)
    cursor.skip_terminators(@c)

    # Past where the lexer named the `(` never closed nothing is its group.
    if unclosed.never_closed(c, text, open: at) && !unclosed.in_reach(c, open: at)
        return ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .error, span: c.tokens[at].span))
    inner = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)
    unclosed.end_group(@c, text: text, open: at)
    return inner

# `[1, 2, 3]`, `[]`, or one element per line (§4.9). The canonical formatter
# picks between the two by length, so the writer never chooses.
function array_literal(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    at = c.pos
    open_ended = unclosed.never_closed(c, text, open: at)
    open = cursor.bump(@c).span
    items: [i64] @ []
    reading: bool @ true

    while reading
        cursor.skip_terminators(@c)

        if cursor.at(c, .rbracket) || cursor.at(c, .eof) || open_ended && !unclosed.in_reach(c, open: at)
            reading @ false
        else
            first = c.pos
            items @ items.push(parse_expr(@c, @a, text))

            # A binding or a mutation on a line below a `[` the lexer named
            # never closed is a statement its reach took in (`unclosed`).
            if open_ended && (cursor.at(c, .colon) || cursor.at(c, .at) || cursor.at(c, .eq)) && unclosed.a_line_below(c, text, open: at, at: first)
                unclosed.past_the_reach(@c, open: at)
                reading @ false
            else if !list_line.separator(@c, text: text, closer: .rbracket, what: "element", open_ended: open_ended, open: at)
                reading @ false

    close = cursor.current_span(c)
    unclosed.end_literal(@c, text: text, open: at)
    return ast.push_expr(
        @a.exprs,
        ast.Expr(kind: .array(elements: items), span: token.Span(start: open.start, end: close.end))
    )

# `{ "mario": 30 }`, `{}`, or one entry per line.
function map_literal(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    at = c.pos
    open_ended = unclosed.never_closed(c, text, open: at)
    open = cursor.bump(@c).span
    entries: [ast.MapEntry] @ []
    reading: bool @ true

    while reading
        cursor.skip_terminators(@c)

        if cursor.at(c, .rbrace) || cursor.at(c, .eof) || open_ended && !unclosed.in_reach(c, open: at)
            reading @ false
        else
            key = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)
            _ = line_end.expect_after(
                @c,
                text: text,
                kind: .colon,
                code: "expected_map_entry_colon",
                what: "`:` between a map's key and its value"
            )
            value = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)
            entries @ entries.push(ast.MapEntry(key: key, value: value))

            if !list_line.separator(@c, text: text, closer: .rbrace, what: "entry", open_ended: open_ended, open: at)
                reading @ false

    close = cursor.current_span(c)
    unclosed.end_literal(@c, text: text, open: at)
    return ast.push_expr(
        @a.exprs,
        ast.Expr(kind: .map(entries: entries), span: token.Span(start: open.start, end: close.end))
    )

# `.plus`, `.num(v: 12)` — a variant case whose type comes from context (§4.5's
# ⇐ mode). Leading dot, because writing `Token.num` would repeat what the
# context already knows.
function case_expr(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    dot = cursor.bump(@c).span

    if !cursor.at(c, .ident)
        if !cursor.at_reported_error(c)
            cursor.error(
                @c,
                code: "expected_case_name",
                message: "expected a variant case name after `.`, found " + cursor.found(c, text) + " — `.plus`, or `.num(v: 12)` with its fields",
                span: cursor.current_span(c)
            )
        return ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .error, span: dot))
    name = cursor.bump(@c).span
    arguments: [ast.Arg] @ []

    if cursor.at(c, .lparen)
        arguments @ call_args(@c, @a, text)
    return ast.push_expr(
        @a.exprs,
        ast.Expr(kind: .variant_case(name: name, args: arguments), span: token.Span(start: dot.start, end: cursor.previous_significant_span(c).end))
    )

## Entry point 3 — the argument list

# `(x, name: y, @l)` — the argument list of a call, of a record construction,
# and of a variant case, which are the same thing (§4.9).
function call_args(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> [ast.Arg]
    arguments: [ast.Arg] @ []
    at = c.pos
    open_ended = unclosed.never_closed(c, text, open: at)
    _ = cursor.bump(@c)
    cursor.skip_terminators(@c)

    if cursor.eat(@c, .rparen) || open_ended && swallowed.body_below(c, text, open: at)
        return arguments
    reading: bool @ true

    while reading
        cursor.skip_terminators(@c)

        if cursor.at(c, .rparen)
            # Reached only after a comma: `f(a, b,)`.
            list_line.trailing_comma(@c, cursor.previous_significant_span(c))
            reading @ false
        else if open_ended && !unclosed.in_reach(c, open: at)
            reading @ false
        else
            first = c.pos
            name: token.Span? @ fail(code: "unnamed", msg: "a bare argument")

            if cursor.at(c, .ident) && cursor.peek(c, 1) == .colon
                name @ ok(cursor.bump(@c).span)
                _ = cursor.bump(@c)
            mutable = cursor.eat(@c, .at)
            value = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)

            # A binding or a mutation on a line below a `(` the lexer named
            # never closed is a statement its reach took in (`unclosed`).
            if open_ended && (cursor.at(c, .at) || cursor.at(c, .eq)) && unclosed.a_line_below(c, text, open: at, at: first)
                unclosed.past_the_reach(@c, open: at)
                reading @ false
                continue

            # `f(@g(x))`, `f(@Bag(items: []))`: the callee copies its result
            # back into the argument (§4.8), so `@` must name where that
            # copy goes — the three shapes the left of `@` accepts, and the
            # resolver then holds the root to the same rule (`not_mutable`).
            # Accepted until 2026-09-03 (M-robustness-guards step 1).
            if mutable && !is_place(a, value)
                cursor.error(
                    @c,
                    code: "not_a_place",
                    message: "only a name, a field or an element can be passed as `@` — the callee copies its result back into the argument, so `@` must name where the value goes",
                    span: a.exprs[value].span
                )

            # A value, then `@`: `f(a @ n)` read as the `f(a: @n)` it may mean,
            # and anything else as the `,` left out before the next argument,
            # each told once (`parse/argument_marker`).
            if cursor.at(c, .at) && argument_marker.a_name_before(a, value: value, labelled: !name.is_err(), marked: mutable)
                named = a.exprs[value].span
                argument_marker.misplaced(@c, value_end: named.end)
                repaired = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)
                arguments @ arguments.push(ast.Arg(name: ok(named), mutable: true, value: repaired))
            else
                arguments @ arguments.push(ast.Arg(name: name, mutable: mutable, value: value))

            if cursor.at(c, .at)
                argument_marker.comma_missing(@c, text: text, value_end: a.exprs[value].span.end)
            else if open_ended && unclosed.ends_at_the_line(c)
                reading @ false
            else
                cursor.skip_terminators(@c)
                reading @ cursor.eat(@c, .comma)

    unclosed.end_arguments(@c, text: text, open: at)
    return arguments

## The statement half of the knot

# `function f():` — the Python suite colon, the one autopilot slip panel 018's
# blind experiment pre-registered above every other (12-18% of files): where
# it is the habit, and how it is said, is `parse/colon_habit`'s.
function eat_python_colon(@c: cursor.Cursor)
    colon_habit.eat(@c)

# The indented body of a declaration, a loop, an `if` or a `match` arm, after
# the head that began at `head`. A failure is `.is_err()` and the caller gives
# up on that construct — where Rust returns `Option<Block>`. What a head that
# failed owes its body, and one in braces, is `parse/opening`'s (defect 131).
function block(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, owner: str, head: opening.Mark) -> ast.Block?
    failed = opening.refused(c, head)
    eat_python_colon(@c)
    found = opening.body_follows(@c, text, head: head, failed: failed, missing: opening.missing_body(owner))

    return match found
        .indented => ok(indented_block(@c, @a, text))
        .braced b => ok(in_braces(@c, @a, text, close: b.close))
        .tabbed   => ok(tabbed_block(@c, @a, text))
        .none     => fail(code: "missing_body", msg: owner)

# The statements of the block whose indent is under the cursor; a block one
# deeper, which no head opens, named and read (`parse/orphans`).
function indented_block(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> ast.Block
    open = cursor.bump(@c).span
    stmts: [i64] @ []
    reading: bool @ true

    while reading
        cursor.skip_terminators(@c)
        k = cursor.current_kind(c)

        if k == .dedent
            _ = cursor.bump(@c)
            reading @ false
        else if k == .eof
            reading @ false
        else if k == .indent
            orphans.read(@c)
            hanging(@c, @a, text)
        else
            stmts @ stmts.push(statement(@c, @a, text))

    return ast.Block(span: token.Span(start: open.end, end: cursor.previous_significant_span(c).end), stmts: stmts)

# A body in braces, its `{` under the cursor and its `}` at `close`, or the lines a
# head's bracket swallowed, to `close`, read as `parse/braced_lines` lays them out.
function in_braces(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, close: i64) -> ast.Block
    lines: cursor.Cursor @ braced_lines.view_to(c, text, close: close)
    body = indented_block(@lines, @a, text)
    braced_lines.took_to(c: @c, view: lines, close: close)
    return body

# The statements of a body whose margin holds a tab (`opening.tabbed_line`),
# and of a block below them, which the lexer opened one level too deep.
function tabbed_block(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> ast.Block
    open = cursor.current_span(c)
    stmts: [i64] @ []

    while opening.tabbed_line(c, text) || cursor.at(c, .indent)
        if cursor.at(c, .indent)
            for s in indented_block(@c, @a, text).stmts
                stmts @ stmts.push(s)
        else
            stmts @ stmts.push(statement(@c, @a, text))

    return ast.Block(span: token.Span(start: open.start, end: cursor.previous_significant_span(c).end), stmts: stmts)

# A block below a line that already said what is wrong: read for its own
# mistakes (defect 130), named first where that line can open none
# (`statement_end.orphan_below`); arms as arms (`headless.arms_below`), and
# braces that end the line as the block they stand for (`parse/brace_habit`).
function hanging(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str)
    if cursor.at(c, .indent) && headless.arms_below(c)
        _ = arms_of(@c, @a, text, tabbed: false)
    else if cursor.at(c, .indent)
        _ = indented_block(@c, @a, text)
    else if brace_habit.opens_here(c, text)
        _ = in_braces(@c, @a, text, close: brace_habit.closer(c, open: c.pos))
    else if opening.tabbed_line(c, text)
        _ = tabbed_block(@c, @a, text)

# A failed declaration head begun at `from`: its line dropped, its body read.
function abandon(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, from: i64)
    brace_habit.drop_rest_of_line(@c, text, from: from)
    hanging(@c, @a, text)

# The block below a line whose head, the token at `word`, nothing reads (`parse/headless`).
function under(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, word: i64)
    left = headless.read_below(@c, @a, text, word: word)

    if left == .declarations
        declarations(@c, @a, text)
    else if left == .statements
        hanging(@c, @a, text)

# A block of declarations under such a line, `class Q` over `function f()`,
# indented or in braces: each line's head by `parse/heads`, its body by `block`.
function declarations(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str)
    if brace_habit.opens_here(c, text)
        lines: cursor.Cursor @ braced_lines.laid_out(c, text)
        declarations(@lines, @a, text)
        braced_lines.took(c: @c, view: lines)
        return
    _ = cursor.bump(@c)
    owed: heads.Owed @ heads.next(@c, @a, text)

    while owed.owes != .done
        if owed.owes == .body
            _ = block(@c, @a, text: text, owner: owed.owner, head: owed.head).is_err()
        else if owed.owes == .abandoned
            abandon(@c, @a, text, from: owed.head.pos)
        else
            _ = statement(@c, @a, text)
        owed @ heads.next(@c, @a, text)

function statement(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    start = cursor.current_span(c)
    from = c.pos
    said = cursor.diagnostic_count(c)
    exprs = a.exprs.len()
    kind: ast.StmtKind @ statement_kind(@c, @a, text)
    return finish(@c, @a, text, start: start, from: from, kind: kind, said: said, exprs: exprs)

function statement_kind(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> ast.StmtKind
    k = cursor.current_kind(c)

    # An `else` opening a statement inside a bracket the lexer named never
    # closed is its `if`'s, whose block the bracket laid out as none: passed
    # over, the rest of its line read (`unclosed.inside_an_open_bracket`).
    if k == .kw_else && unclosed.inside_an_open_bracket(c, text, from: c.pos)
        _ = cursor.bump(@c)

        if cursor.at(c, .terminator) || cursor.at(c, .eof) || cursor.at(c, .dedent)
            return .error
        return statement_kind(@c, @a, text)

    if k == .kw_return
        return return_stmt(@c, @a, text)

    if k == .kw_break
        _ = cursor.bump(@c)
        return .break_stmt

    if k == .kw_continue
        _ = cursor.bump(@c)
        return .continue_stmt

    if k == .kw_assert
        _ = cursor.bump(@c)
        return .assert_stmt(value: parse_expr(@c, @a, text))

    if k == .kw_for
        return for_stmt(@c, @a, text)

    if k == .kw_while
        return while_stmt(@c, @a, text)

    # `x = …` and `v: … @ …` are the two declaration shapes; everything else
    # starting with a name is an expression, and possibly the place of a
    # mutation.
    if k == .ident && cursor.peek(c, 1) == .eq
        return bind_stmt(@c, @a, text)

    if k == .ident && cursor.peek(c, 1) == .colon
        return annotated(@c, @a, text)
    return expression_or_mutation(@c, @a, text)

# Every statement ends at its line end, consumed here; what that owes is
# `parse/statement_end`'s, once a literal that never closed, its last
# operand, is taken.
function finish(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, start: token.Span, from: i64, kind: ast.StmtKind, said: i64, exprs: i64) -> i64
    if !cursor.refused_since(c, said) && statement_end.refused_operand(c, a, text, exprs: exprs)
        _ = cursor.bump(@c)

    # The last significant token, never a trailing comment, is what the caret underlines.
    span = token.Span(start: start.start, end: cursor.previous_significant_span(c).end)
    below = statement_end.close(@c, text, from: from, failed: cursor.refused_since(c, said))

    if below == .refused
        under(@c, @a, text, word: from)
    else if below == .read
        hanging(@c, @a, text)
    else if below == .rest
        _ = statement(@c, @a, text)
    return ast.push_stmt(@a.stmts, ast.Stmt(kind: kind, span: span))

# `return e`, or bare `return` where the function returns nothing.
function return_stmt(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> ast.StmtKind
    _ = cursor.bump(@c)

    if cursor.at(c, .terminator) || cursor.at(c, .dedent) || cursor.at(c, .eof)
        return .return_stmt(value: fail(code: "bare", msg: "no value"))
    return .return_stmt(value: ok(parse_expr(@c, @a, text)))

# `while cond` — the condition loop (§4.7; panel 018 split it out of the old
# two-grammar `for`), or C's `do`'s tail (`parse/loop_habit`).
function while_stmt(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> ast.StmtKind
    head = opening.mark(c)
    _ = cursor.bump(@c)
    cond = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)
    colon_habit.read_below(@c, text)

    if loop_habit.do_tail(c, text, head: head.pos)
        return .error
    body = block(@c, @a, text: text, owner: "a `while`", head: head)

    if body.is_err()
        return .error
    return .while_stmt(cond: cond, block: body.must())

# `for x in xs` — iteration only (§4.7). A condition after `for` is met with
# *use `while`*, as sure as the rest reads as its condition (`parse/loop_habit`).
function for_stmt(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> ast.StmtKind
    head = opening.mark(c)
    keyword = cursor.bump(@c).span

    if cursor.at(c, .ident) && cursor.peek(c, 1) == .kw_in
        name = cursor.bump(@c).span
        _ = cursor.bump(@c)
        iterable = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)
        colon_habit.read_below(@c, text)
        body = block(@c, @a, text: text, owner: "a `for`", head: head)

        if body.is_err()
            return .error
        return .for_in(name: name, iterable: iterable, block: body.must())

    if !cursor.at_reported_error(c)
        read: cursor.Cursor @ c
        scratch: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
        _ = parse_expr(@read, @scratch, text)
        loop_habit.refuse(c: @c, text: text, keyword: keyword, read: read)

    # The rest is not read as the condition it may have been, which refused
    # again at the same column (`for` alone, `for >`, defect 131): one mistake,
    # one diagnostic, and `block` still reads the body.
    cond = ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .error, span: cursor.current_span(c)))
    recovered = block(@c, @a, text: text, owner: "a `for`", head: head)

    if recovered.is_err()
        return .error
    return .while_stmt(cond: cond, block: recovered.must())

## The three line shapes that begin with a name

# `x = 5` — binds once, forever (§4.4). The type is inferred, and inference is
# local: the value on this line decides it (§4.5).
function bind_stmt(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> ast.StmtKind
    name = cursor.bump(@c).span
    _ = cursor.bump(@c)
    return .bind(name: name, ty: fail(code: "inferred", msg: "no written type"), value: parse_expr(@c, @a, text))

# `v: i64 @ 0` — a mutable cell, type mandatory. `xs: [i64] = []` — an
# immutable binding that needs its annotation because an empty literal cannot
# say what it holds (§4.5). A type that failed is one mistake, and what it
# owes the `@` or `=` after it is `parse/annotation`'s (defect 131's class).
function annotated(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> ast.StmtKind
    name = cursor.bump(@c).span
    _ = cursor.bump(@c)
    typed = opening.mark(c)
    ty = type.parse_type(@c, @a, text)
    annotation.past_a_refused_type(@c, typed)

    if cursor.eat(@c, .at)
        return .declare(name: name, ty: ty, value: parse_expr(@c, @a, text))

    if cursor.eat(@c, .eq)
        return .bind(name: name, ty: ok(ty), value: parse_expr(@c, @a, text))
    annotation.no_binding_symbol(@c, text, typed)
    return .error

# Everything else: an expression alone on its line, or the place on the left of
# an `@`; `@v = e`, a sigil before a name, is `prefixed`.
function expression_or_mutation(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> ast.StmtKind
    if at_prefix.before_a_place(c)
        return prefixed(@c, @a, text)
    value = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)

    # A block that just closed ended the statement: an `@` below it opens the
    # next one, never this one's mutation (`parse/at_prefix`). A place that
    # could not be read at all was told by what refused it, `@ 5` at its `@`.
    if cursor.previous_kind(c) == .dedent || !cursor.eat(@c, .at)
        return .expr_stmt(value: value)

    if !is_place(a, value) && a.exprs[value].kind != .error
        cursor.error(
            @c,
            code: "not_a_place",
            message: "only a name, a field or an element can be mutated — the left of `@` must name where the value goes",
            span: a.exprs[value].span
        )
    return .mutate(place: value, value: parse_expr(@c, @a, text))

# `@v = e`, told once at its `@` and read as the statement the line is with the
# sigil gone (`parse/at_prefix`): the mutation `v @ e`, past its `=` or `@`,
# the discard, the declaration, or the line as it stands.
function prefixed(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> ast.StmtKind
    return match at_prefix.told(@c, text)
        .declaration => annotated(@c, @a, text)
        .discard     => bind_stmt(@c, @a, text)
        .value       => expression_or_mutation(@c, @a, text)
        .mutation =>
            place = postfix(@c, @a, text)
            _ = cursor.bump(@c)
            .mutate(place: place, value: parse_expr(@c, @a, text))

# A PLACE: a name, or a field or index path rooted at one. Since Heroes has no
# references, every place has exactly one root — which is what makes panel 010's
# alias test a comparison of roots rather than a dataflow analysis.
function is_place(a: ast.Ast, at: i64) -> bool
    return match a.exprs[at].kind
        .name    => true
        .field f => is_place(a, f.base)
        # A field's NAME is a value, never a place: `Point::x @ 1` is refused here
        # rather than by a message about records (panel 132).

        .field_name                                                                            => false
        .index i                                                                               => is_place(a, i.base)
        .int_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .char_lit | .bool_lit | .null_ptr | .hole => false
        .unary | .binary | .call | .method | .variant_case                                     => false
        .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error                            => false

## `if` and `match`, which are expressions (§4.7)

# `if cond` + block, then any number of `else if`, then an optional `else`. No
# parentheses around the condition (§4.15) and no truthiness: the condition must
# be `bool`, checked by the type checker.
function if_expr(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    head = opening.mark(c)
    start = cursor.bump(@c).span
    branches: [ast.Branch] @ []
    cond = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)
    colon_habit.read_below(@c, text)
    first = block(@c, @a, text: text, owner: "an `if`", head: head)
    whole = !first.is_err()

    if whole
        branches @ branches.push(ast.Branch(cond: cond, block: first.must()))
    otherwise: ast.Block? @ fail(code: "no_else", msg: "the `if` has no `else`")

    # `else` closes the `if`'s block, so the cursor is back at this level. A
    # branch whose body was missing leaves it at the line below too, and the
    # `else` there is still this `if`'s: read on its own it was a second
    # mistake, at the column of the first (defect 130).
    chaining: bool @ true

    # And `elif` opening its line is read as the `else if` its certain swap
    # writes, where it cost `unexpected_block` and a stray `else` beside the
    # lexer's `reserved_word` (defect 133's class).
    while chaining && (cursor.at(c, .kw_else) || cursor.at(c, .error) && opening.at_elif(c, text))
        branch_head = opening.mark(c)
        swapped = cursor.bump(@c).kind == .error

        if swapped || cursor.eat(@c, .kw_if)
            more_cond = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)
            colon_habit.read_below(@c, text)
            more = block(@c, @a, text: text, owner: "an `else if`", head: branch_head)

            if !more.is_err()
                read = more.must()
                branches @ branches.push(ast.Branch(cond: more_cond, block: read))
        else
            colon_habit.read_below(@c, text)
            last = block(@c, @a, text: text, owner: "an `else`", head: branch_head)

            if !last.is_err()
                otherwise @ last
            chaining @ false

    if !whole
        return ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .error, span: start))
    end: token.Span @ branches[branches.len() - 1].block.span

    if !otherwise.is_err()
        end @ otherwise.must().span
    return ast.push_expr(
        @a.exprs,
        ast.Expr(kind: .if_expr(branches: branches, otherwise: otherwise), span: token.Span(start: start.start, end: end.end))
    )

# `match e` + one arm per line, indented; in braces, the brace habit, said
# once and its arms read (`parse/braced_arms`).
function match_expr(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str) -> i64
    head = opening.mark(c)
    start = cursor.bump(@c).span
    scrutinee = parse_expr(@c, @a, text)
    failed = opening.refused(c, head)
    colon_habit.read_below(@c, text)
    eat_python_colon(@c)
    brace = brace_habit.ending(c, text)
    arms: [ast.Arm] @ []

    if brace >= 0
        lines: cursor.Cursor @ braced_arms.lines_of(@c, text, head: head, failed: failed, brace: brace)
        arms @ arms_of(@lines, @a, text, tabbed: false)
        braced_arms.taken(c: @c, lines: lines)
    else
        found = opening.body_follows(@c, text, head: head, failed: failed, missing: opening.missing_arms())

        if found == .none
            misplaced(@c, @a, text, head: head.pos)
            return ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .error, span: start))
        arms @ arms_of(@c, @a, text, tabbed: found == .tabbed)
    span = token.Span(start: start.start, end: cursor.previous_significant_span(c).end)
    return ast.push_expr(@a.exprs, ast.Expr(kind: .match_expr(scrutinee: scrutinee, arms: arms), span: span))

# The arms of a `match` at token `head` with none under it, where they stand
# misplaced, at its own margin or past its block: read as arms, their one
# report the arms named missing (`parse/margin_arms`).
function misplaced(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, head: i64)
    past = margin_arms.past_the_block(c, text)

    if margin_arms.below(c, head)
        _ = arms_of(@c, @a, text, tabbed: false)
    else if past >= 0
        lines: cursor.Cursor @ margin_arms.on(c, at: past)
        _ = arms_of(@lines, @a, text, tabbed: false)
        margin_arms.taken(c: @c, lines: lines, from: past)

# The arms of a `match`: the block whose indent is under the cursor, or the
# lines whose margin holds a tab and a block below them, as `tabbed_block`;
# with neither, those at its own margin that hold an arm (`parse/margin_arms`).
function arms_of(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, tabbed: bool) -> [ast.Arm]
    at_margin = !tabbed && !cursor.at(c, .indent)

    if !tabbed && !at_margin
        _ = cursor.bump(@c)
    arms: [ast.Arm] @ []
    reading: bool @ true
    failed: bool @ false
    in_tabs: bool @ tabbed
    run: i64 @ -1

    while reading
        cursor.skip_terminators(@c)
        k = cursor.current_kind(c)

        if in_tabs && k == .indent
            _ = cursor.bump(@c)
            in_tabs @ false
        else if in_tabs && !opening.tabbed_line(c, text)
            reading @ false
        else if failed && !in_tabs && opening.tabbed_line(c, text)
            # The body of a failed arm whose `=>` the lexer joined to it.
            hanging(@c, @a, text)
        else if at_margin && margin_arms.end_here(c, @run)
            reading @ false
        else if k == .dedent
            _ = cursor.bump(@c)
            reading @ false
        else if k == .eof
            reading @ false
        else if k == .indent
            # Where an arm should start: below a failed arm, its body, read and
            # not named; below a whole one, an orphan, named and its lines read
            # as every orphan's are (`parse/orphans`; it went unnamed, and
            # `0 => print(1)` over `print(99)` printed 1). Else the loop spins:
            # `skip_line` stops BEFORE an indent (panel 014).
            if !failed
                orphans.read(@c)
            hanging(@c, @a, text)
        else
            from = c.pos
            one = arm(@c, @a, text, own_block: !at_margin && !tabbed)
            failed @ one.is_err()

            match one
                # A pattern or `=>` that failed consumed nothing reusable, and the
                # rest of its line goes, a line joined to it too (130, 166).
                .err => cursor.drop_rest_of_line(@c, from: from)

                # A body that failed has already dropped the rest of its line
                # (`finish` does it for every statement), so the loop must NOT
                # skip again — that ate the arm below it.
                .ok taken => arms @ arms.push(taken)

            # An arm whose `=>` stood past the arms' dedent ended their block.
            reading @ !broken_arm.crossed_its_dedent(c, from: from)

    return arms

# One arm: patterns joined by `|`, `=>`, then one statement on the same line or
# a block below it (§4.7, panel 014).
function arm(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, own_block: bool) -> ast.Arm?
    head = opening.mark(c)
    start = cursor.current_span(c)
    arm_line.spaced_sign(@c, text)
    lead = operator_arm.lead(@c, @a, text)

    if lead.is_err()
        return fail(code: "no_pattern", msg: "the arm has no pattern")
    patterns: [ast.Pattern] @ lead.must().patterns
    joining: bool @ true

    while joining && cursor.eat(@c, .pipe)
        more = arm_pattern.read(@c, @a, text)

        if more.is_err()
            return fail(code: "no_pattern", msg: "a joined pattern is missing")
        patterns @ patterns.push(more.must())

    # An arm broken before its `=>` goes on at it (`parse/broken_arm`).
    if !broken_arm.arrow(@c, text, own_block: own_block)
        return fail(code: "no_arrow", msg: "the arm has no `=>`")

    # One statement, or a block — the same two shapes every other body has
    # (panel 014). Going through `statement` is what gives an arm `assert`,
    # `break` and `return`, and what stops the printer from having a second path
    # that cannot render a block under a control head. Never across a line
    # end: an arm's body is on its line or in the block below it.
    body: ast.ArmBody @ .one_stmt(at: 0)

    if cursor.at(c, .indent) || opening.tabbed_line(c, text) || braced_arms.body_opens(c, text)
        inner = block(@c, @a, text: text, owner: "a `match` arm", head: head)

        if inner.is_err()
            return fail(code: "no_body", msg: "the arm's block is missing")
        body @ .block(block: inner.must())
    else
        at = statement(@c, @a, text)
        arm_body.reject_declaration(@c, a, text, at)
        body @ .one_stmt(at: at)
    broken_arm.close_its_block(@c, from: head.pos)

    # A pattern begun with an operator was told, and the arm read past it for
    # its own mistakes (`parse/operator_arm`).
    if lead.must().told
        return fail(code: "no_pattern", msg: "the arm's pattern began with an operator")
    return ok(
        ast.Arm(patterns: patterns, body: body, span: token.Span(start: start.start, end: cursor.previous_significant_span(c).end))
    )

## Tests — the precedence table, read back off real token streams

function tok(kind: token.TokenKind, start: i64, end: i64) -> token.Token
    return token.Token(kind: kind, span: token.Span(start: start, end: end))

# The tree, written back out fully parenthesised. A precedence table is only
# testable through what it BUILDS, and this is the cheapest way to see it.
# `(interp f"a { n } b")`: the pieces verbatim and each hole re-printed, so a
# call inside a hole shows in the dump as it shows anywhere.
function interp_render(a: ast.Ast, pieces: [token.Span], holes: [i64], text: str) -> str
    parts: [str] @ ["(interp"]
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < pieces.len()
        parts @ parts.push(" " + cursor.slice_of(text, pieces[i]))

        if i < holes.len()
            parts @ parts.push(" " + render(a, holes[i], text))
        i @ i + 1

    parts @ parts.push(")")
    return parts.join("")

function render(a: ast.Ast, at: i64, text: str) -> str
    node = a.exprs[at]

    return match node.kind
        .binary b => "(" + render(a, b.left, text) + " " + operators.spelling(b.op) + " " + render(a, b.right, text) + ")"
        .unary u      => "(" + operators.unary_spelling(u.op) + render(a, u.operand, text) + ")"
        .field f      => "(" + render(a, f.base, text) + "." + cursor.slice_of(text, f.name) + ")"
        .field_name f => "(" + render(a, f.base, text) + "::" + cursor.slice_of(text, f.name) + ")"
        .index i      => "(" + render(a, i.base, text) + "[" + render(a, i.index, text) + "])"
        .try_expr t   => "(" + render(a, t.value, text) + "?)"
        .call c       => "(call " + render(a, c.callee, text) + "/" + c.args.len().to_str() + ")"
        .method m => "(" + render(a, m.receiver, text) + "." + cursor.slice_of(text, m.name) + "()/" + m.args.len().to_str() + ")"
        .variant_case v                                          => "." + cursor.slice_of(text, v.name)
        .array ar                                                => "[" + ar.elements.len().to_str() + "]"
        .map mp                                                  => "{" + mp.entries.len().to_str() + "}"
        .interp ip                                               => interp_render(a, ip.pieces, ip.holes, text)
        .int_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .char_lit | .bool_lit => cursor.slice_of(text, node.span)
        .name                                                    => cursor.slice_of(text, node.span)
        .null_ptr                                                => "nullptr"
        .hole                                                    => "???"
        .if_expr | .match_expr                                   => "<control>"
        .error                                                   => "<error>"

test "multiplication binds tighter than addition, and both are left-associative"
    # 1 + 2 * 3
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.int_lit, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.plus, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.star, start: 6, end: 7)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 8, end: 9)
        tok(.eof, start: 9, end: 9)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at = parse_expr(@c, @a, "1 + 2 * 3")
    assert render(a, at, "1 + 2 * 3") == "(1 + (2 * 3))"

    # 1 - 2 - 3 groups left
    left: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.int_lit, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.minus, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.minus, start: 6, end: 7)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 8, end: 9)
        tok(.eof, start: 9, end: 9)
    ]
    d: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(left)
    b: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at2 = parse_expr(@d, @b, "1 - 2 - 3")
    assert render(b, at2, "1 - 2 - 3") == "((1 - 2) - 3)"

test "comparisons sit above &&, so a < b && c needs no parentheses"
    # a < b && c
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.lt, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.ident, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.and_and, start: 6, end: 8)
        tok(.ident, start: 9, end: 10)
        tok(.eof, start: 10, end: 10)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at = parse_expr(@c, @a, "a < b && c")
    assert render(a, at, "a < b && c") == "((a < b) && c)"

test "C's own mistake is not inherited: & binds tighter than =="
    # x & 1 == 0  — in C this means x & (1 == 0); here it is (x & 1) == 0
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.amp, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.eq_eq, start: 6, end: 8)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 9, end: 10)
        tok(.eof, start: 10, end: 10)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at = parse_expr(@c, @a, "x & 1 == 0")
    assert render(a, at, "x & 1 == 0") == "((x & 1) == 0)"

test "the suffixes chain left to right"
    # xs[0].f
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 2)
        tok(.lbracket, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 3, end: 4)
        tok(.rbracket, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.dot, start: 5, end: 6)
        tok(.ident, start: 6, end: 7)
        tok(.eof, start: 7, end: 7)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at = parse_expr(@c, @a, "xs[0].f")
    assert render(a, at, "xs[0].f") == "((xs[0]).f)"

    # e? binds tightest of all
    q: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.question, start: 1, end: 2)
        tok(.plus, start: 3, end: 4)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 5, end: 6)
        tok(.eof, start: 6, end: 6)
    ]
    d: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(q)
    b: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at2 = parse_expr(@d, @b, "e? + 1")
    assert render(b, at2, "e? + 1") == "((e?) + 1)"

test "a dot with parentheses is UFCS, without them a field"
    # x.f(y)
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.dot, start: 1, end: 2)
        tok(.ident, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.lparen, start: 3, end: 4)
        tok(.ident, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.rparen, start: 5, end: 6)
        tok(.eof, start: 6, end: 6)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at = parse_expr(@c, @a, "x.f(y)")
    assert render(a, at, "x.f(y)") == "(x.f()/1)"

test "a trailing comma is refused with the deletion as its fix"
    # f(a,)
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.lparen, start: 1, end: 2)
        tok(.ident, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.comma, start: 3, end: 4)
        tok(.rparen, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.eof, start: 5, end: 5)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    _ = parse_expr(@c, @a, "f(a,)")
    assert c.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert c.diagnostics[0].code == "trailing_comma"
    assert c.diagnostics[0].fixes[0].certainty == .certain
    assert c.diagnostics[0].fixes[0].replacement == ""

test "a name before `@` reads as f(a: @n) and as f(a, @n), neither certain"
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.lparen, start: 1, end: 2)
        tok(.ident, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.at, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.ident, start: 6, end: 7)
        tok(.rparen, start: 7, end: 8)
        tok(.eof, start: 8, end: 8)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    _ = parse_expr(@c, @a, "f(a @ n)")
    assert c.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert c.diagnostics[0].code == "misplaced_mutable_marker"
    assert c.diagnostics[0].fixes[0].replacement == ": @"
    assert c.diagnostics[0].fixes[1].replacement == ", @"
    assert c.diagnostics[0].fixes[0].certainty == .guess
    assert c.diagnostics[0].fixes[1].certainty == .guess

test "inside brackets a newline is a separator, not a statement end"
    # [1
    #  2]  — the lexer plants a terminator between them
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.lbracket, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 1, end: 2)
        tok(.terminator, start: 2, end: 2)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 3, end: 4)
        tok(.rbracket, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.eof, start: 5, end: 5)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at = parse_expr(@c, @a, "[1\n2]")
    assert render(a, at, "[1\n2]") == "[2]"
    assert c.diagnostics.len() == 0

test "a pattern's operand never consumes the join, which is what | is there"
    # 1 | 2 in pattern position must stop at the `|`, leaving it for the arm.
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.int_lit, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.pipe, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.eof, start: 5, end: 5)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at = arm_pattern.literal(@c, @a, "1 | 2").must()
    assert render(a, at, "1 | 2") == "1"
    assert cursor.current_kind(c) == .pipe

    # The full expression parser DOES consume it — which is the defect the
    # split prevents.
    d: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    b: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at2 = parse_expr(@d, @b, "1 | 2")
    assert render(b, at2, "1 | 2") == "(1 | 2)"

## Tests — the statement half, and the ring closed

test "the three name-shaped lines are told apart by one token"
    # x = 5
    bind_line: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.eq, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.terminator, start: 5, end: 5)
        tok(.eof, start: 5, end: 5)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(bind_line)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at = statement(@c, @a, "x = 5")

    match a.stmts[at].kind
        .bind b                                                          => assert b.ty.is_err()
        .declare | .mutate | .return_stmt | .break_stmt | .continue_stmt => assert false
        .assert_stmt | .while_stmt | .for_in | .expr_stmt | .error       => assert false

    assert c.diagnostics.len() == 0

    # v @ v + 1 — a mutation, because the place is followed by `@`
    mutate_line: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.at, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.ident, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.plus, start: 6, end: 7)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 8, end: 9)
        tok(.terminator, start: 9, end: 9)
        tok(.eof, start: 9, end: 9)
    ]
    d: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(mutate_line)
    b: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at2 = statement(@d, @b, "v @ v + 1")

    match b.stmts[at2].kind
        .mutate _                                                      => assert true
        .bind | .declare | .return_stmt | .break_stmt | .continue_stmt => assert false
        .assert_stmt | .while_stmt | .for_in | .expr_stmt | .error     => assert false

    assert d.diagnostics.len() == 0

test "only a place can be mutated"
    # f(x) @ 1 — a call is not a place
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.lparen, start: 1, end: 2)
        tok(.ident, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.rparen, start: 3, end: 4)
        tok(.at, start: 5, end: 6)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 7, end: 8)
        tok(.terminator, start: 8, end: 8)
        tok(.eof, start: 8, end: 8)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    _ = statement(@c, @a, "f(x) @ 1")
    assert c.diagnostics[0].code == "not_a_place"

test "the Python colon is eaten with its fix, so the block still parses"
    # while c:
    #     break
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.kw_while, start: 0, end: 5)
        tok(.ident, start: 6, end: 7)
        tok(.colon, start: 7, end: 8)
        tok(.terminator, start: 8, end: 8)
        tok(.indent, start: 9, end: 13)
        tok(.kw_break, start: 13, end: 18)
        tok(.terminator, start: 18, end: 18)
        tok(.dedent, start: 19, end: 19)
        tok(.eof, start: 19, end: 19)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at = statement(@c, @a, "while c:\n    break\n")
    assert c.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert c.diagnostics[0].code == "trailing_colon"
    assert c.diagnostics[0].fixes[0].certainty == .certain

    # And the block still parsed: one habit costs one diagnostic.
    match a.stmts[at].kind
        .while_stmt w                                                 => assert w.block.stmts.len() == 1
        .bind | .declare | .mutate | .return_stmt | .break_stmt       => assert false
        .continue_stmt | .assert_stmt | .for_in | .expr_stmt | .error => assert false

test "for over a condition is met with while, and the certainty says how sure"
    # for !done — no loop variable can be there, so the fix is CERTAIN
    sure: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.kw_for, start: 0, end: 3)
        tok(.bang, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.ident, start: 5, end: 9)
        tok(.terminator, start: 9, end: 9)
        tok(.indent, start: 10, end: 14)
        tok(.kw_break, start: 14, end: 19)
        tok(.terminator, start: 19, end: 19)
        tok(.dedent, start: 20, end: 20)
        tok(.eof, start: 20, end: 20)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(sure)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    _ = statement(@c, @a, "for !done\n    break\n")
    assert c.diagnostics[0].code == "for_missing_in"
    assert c.diagnostics[0].fixes[0].replacement == "while"
    assert c.diagnostics[0].fixes[0].certainty == .certain

    # for x > 0 — `for x of xs` reaches the same state, so it is a GUESS
    unsure: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.kw_for, start: 0, end: 3)
        tok(.ident, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.gt, start: 6, end: 7)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 8, end: 9)
        tok(.terminator, start: 9, end: 9)
        tok(.indent, start: 10, end: 14)
        tok(.kw_break, start: 14, end: 19)
        tok(.terminator, start: 19, end: 19)
        tok(.dedent, start: 20, end: 20)
        tok(.eof, start: 20, end: 20)
    ]
    d: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(unsure)
    b: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    _ = statement(@d, @b, "for x > 0\n    break\n")
    assert d.diagnostics[0].fixes[0].certainty == .guess

    # for (i = 0) — C's loop: `while (i = 0)` refuses its `=`, so it is no
    # loop and the fix is a GUESS, though `(` can begin an expression
    # (`parse/loop_habit`, lane X3, 2026-09-29).
    c_style: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.kw_for, start: 0, end: 3)
        tok(.lparen, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.ident, start: 5, end: 6)
        tok(.eq, start: 7, end: 8)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 9, end: 10)
        tok(.rparen, start: 10, end: 11)
        tok(.terminator, start: 11, end: 11)
        tok(.indent, start: 12, end: 16)
        tok(.kw_break, start: 16, end: 21)
        tok(.terminator, start: 21, end: 21)
        tok(.dedent, start: 22, end: 22)
        tok(.eof, start: 22, end: 22)
    ]
    e: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(c_style)
    f: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    _ = statement(@e, @f, "for (i = 0)\n    break\n")
    assert e.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert e.diagnostics[0].code == "for_missing_in"
    assert e.diagnostics[0].fixes[0].certainty == .guess

test "the ring is closed: if is an expression with a block inside it"
    # x = if c
    #         1
    #     else
    #         2
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.eq, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.kw_if, start: 4, end: 6)
        tok(.ident, start: 7, end: 8)
        tok(.terminator, start: 8, end: 8)
        tok(.indent, start: 9, end: 17)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 17, end: 18)
        tok(.terminator, start: 18, end: 18)
        tok(.dedent, start: 19, end: 19)
        tok(.kw_else, start: 19, end: 23)
        tok(.terminator, start: 23, end: 23)
        tok(.indent, start: 24, end: 32)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 32, end: 33)
        tok(.terminator, start: 33, end: 33)
        tok(.dedent, start: 34, end: 34)
        tok(.eof, start: 34, end: 34)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    at = statement(@c, @a, "x = if c\n        1\n    else\n        2\n")
    assert c.diagnostics.len() == 0

    match a.stmts[at].kind
        .bind b =>
            match a.exprs[b.value].kind
                .if_expr i =>
                    assert i.branches.len() == 1
                    assert !i.otherwise.is_err()
                .int_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .char_lit | .bool_lit  => assert false
                .null_ptr | .name | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field | .field_name => assert false
                .index | .call | .method | .variant_case | .array                   => assert false
                .map | .try_expr | .match_expr | .error                             => assert false
        .declare | .mutate | .return_stmt | .break_stmt | .continue_stmt => assert false
        .assert_stmt | .while_stmt | .for_in | .expr_stmt | .error       => assert false

test "a match arm may not declare a name where nothing can read it"
    # match e
    #     .a => x = 5
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.kw_match, start: 0, end: 5)
        tok(.ident, start: 6, end: 7)
        tok(.terminator, start: 7, end: 7)
        tok(.indent, start: 8, end: 12)
        tok(.dot, start: 12, end: 13)
        tok(.ident, start: 13, end: 14)
        tok(.fat_arrow, start: 15, end: 17)
        tok(.ident, start: 18, end: 19)
        tok(.eq, start: 20, end: 21)
        tok(.int_lit, start: 22, end: 23)
        tok(.terminator, start: 23, end: 23)
        tok(.dedent, start: 24, end: 24)
        tok(.eof, start: 24, end: 24)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    _ = parse_expr(@c, @a, "match e\n    .a => x = 5\n")
    codes: [str] @ []

    for d in c.diagnostics
        codes @ codes.push(d.code)

    assert codes == ["declaration_in_arm"]

test "only a place can be passed as @"
    # f(@g(x)) — a call's result has nowhere to receive the copy-out
    stream: [token.Token] = [
        tok(.ident, start: 0, end: 1)
        tok(.lparen, start: 1, end: 2)
        tok(.at, start: 2, end: 3)
        tok(.ident, start: 3, end: 4)
        tok(.lparen, start: 4, end: 5)
        tok(.ident, start: 5, end: 6)
        tok(.rparen, start: 6, end: 7)
        tok(.rparen, start: 7, end: 8)
        tok(.terminator, start: 8, end: 8)
        tok(.eof, start: 8, end: 8)
    ]
    c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
    a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
    _ = statement(@c, @a, "f(@g(x))")
    assert c.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert c.diagnostics[0].code == "not_a_place"

# `opening.begins_an_expression` was what a `certain` *use `while`* rested on
# (defect 131's third round) until lane X3, 2026-09-29, when the rest of the
# line came to be read as the `while`'s condition (`parse/loop_habit`). It is
# still a second copy of what `unary` and `primary` accept, and this is where
# the two are held to agree, token by token. `.error` is the lexer's and
# `.comment` never reaches the grammar.
test "an expression begins exactly where begins_an_expression says it can"
    kinds: [token.TokenKind] = [
        .ident
        .int_lit
        .float_lit
        .str_lit
        .str_piece
        .char_lit
        .kw_constant
        .kw_function
        .kw_record
        .kw_variant
        .kw_match
        .kw_if
        .kw_else
        .kw_for
        .kw_while
        .kw_in
        .kw_break
        .kw_continue
        .kw_return
        .kw_test
        .kw_assert
        .kw_extern
        .kw_nullptr
        .kw_use
        .kw_true
        .kw_false
        .kw_fail
        .eq
        .at
        .colon
        .colon_colon
        .comma
        .dot
        .question
        .hole
        .arrow
        .fat_arrow
        .pipe
        .lparen
        .rparen
        .lbracket
        .rbracket
        .lbrace
        .rbrace
        .plus
        .minus
        .star
        .slash
        .percent
        .eq_eq
        .bang_eq
        .lt
        .le
        .gt
        .ge
        .and_and
        .or_or
        .bang
        .amp
        .caret
        .tilde
        .shl
        .shr
        .terminator
        .indent
        .dedent
        .eof
    ]

    for k in kinds
        stream: [token.Token] = [tok(k, start: 0, end: 1), tok(.eof, start: 1, end: 1)]
        c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.new_cursor(stream)
        a: ast.Ast @ ast.new_ast()
        _ = parse_expr(@c, @a, "x ")
        refused_first = c.diagnostics.len() > 0 && c.diagnostics[0].code == "expected_expression" && c.diagnostics[0].span.start == 0
        assert refused_first == !opening.begins_an_expression(k)
